{"abstract":"Connected with various resurgent and decolonizing projects, Canada has seen a surge of renaming and Indigenous land acknowledgement, which draw attention to Indigenous territories that have been overwritten through colonial naming practices. While renaming practices and land acknowledgments are contested for having merely representational effects, they may also be linked with decolonizing efforts. Our paper explores subversive (re)naming practices afforded by the free-form location identifying function on Twitter’s user profiles. It then draws a connection to issue-alignment in relation to the contested Trans Mountain pipeline as a means of considering to what extent toponymic selection is linked with actual issue alignment within the colonial context of resource extraction in Canada. We apply a mixed methods approach, based in digital methods that work with Twitter’s user profile location category. We extend our analysis through a qualitative reading of key subsets of the Twitter data, using a grounded theory approach to identify prevalent themes. In keeping with the anti-colonial nature of the tweets, we resist colonial categorization of the data and instead share an “un-typology” of Twitter toponyms, which we then connect to various expressions of anti-pipeline positioning. These mixed methods help us explore the entanglement of representational toponymic significance, infrastructural, in relation to the platform and the colonial nature of geolocational regimes online, and grounded, in relation to issue expression regarding the Trans Mountain pipeline.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Karsgaard, Carrie","MacDonald, Maggie","Hockenhull, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382728"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["Canada"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Alberta","University of Toronto"],"keyword":["Decolonization","Digital Methods","Settler colonialism","Social media","Twitter","critical toponymy","land acknowledgements"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"rename and resist settler colonialism land acknowledgments and twitter s toponymic politics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5210/fm.v26i2.11454","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382728","identifier":[{"id":"85159207138","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-617e0a9c-71cf-4423-9580-557f5b7c11bc"],"keyword":["critical toponymy","decolonization","digital methods","land acknowledgements","settler colonialism","social media","twitter"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-D7687E9B196C4C770396A6F0E50D2692"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/93902049/Karsgaard_et_al._2021_Rename_and_resist_settler_colonialism_Land_acknow.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5210/fm.v26i2.11454","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.uic.edu/ojs/index.php/fm/article/download/11454/10061","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"ca","key":"university of alberta","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Alberta","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"university of toronto","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Toronto","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carrie","key":"karsgaardpau","last":"Karsgaard","name":"Karsgaard, Carrie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maggie","key":"macdonaldpau","last":"MacDonald","name":"MacDonald, Maggie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5210/fm.v26i2.11454","issn":["13960458","13960466"],"issn_org":["13960466"],"issue":"2","journal":"First Monday","publisher":"First Monday Editorial Group","status":"p","title":"First Monday","volume":"26"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85159207138"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-617e0a9c-71cf-4423-9580-557f5b7c11bc"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Rename and resist settler colonialism: Land acknowledgments and Twitter’s toponymic politics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382728","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This paper studies data work in an organizational context, and suggests speculative data work as a useful concept and the speculative dashboard as a design concept, to better understand and support cooperative work. Drawing on fieldwork in a Danish public sector organisation, the paper identifies and conceptualizes the speculative data work performed around processes of digitalization and the push to become data-driven. The speculative dashboard is proposed as a design concept and opportunity for design, using practices from speculative design and research to facilitate speculation about data?its sources, visualizations, practices and infrastructures. It does so by hacking the 'genre' of the business intelligence data dashboard, and using it as a framework for the juxtaposition of different kinds of data, facilitating and encouraging speculation on alternative visions for data types and use. The paper contributes an empirical study of organizational use of and attitudes towards data, informing a novel design method and concept for co-speculating on alternative visions of and for organizational data.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Hockenhull, Michael","Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383945"],"event":{"acronym":"CSCW","title":"ACM Conference on Computer Supported Cooperative Work"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Business intelligence","Data visualization","Data work","Ethnography","Speculative design"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"speculative data work amp dashboards designing alternative data visions","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85672321/SDWD_pre_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383945","identifier":[{"id":"85175611527","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b7937331-5ab4-4ae6-810e-90c2cfc52cb4"],"keyword":["business intelligence","data visualization","data work","ethnography","speculative design"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-CAC9F1047EDC8DD872A583782A18F25F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/85672321/SDWD_pre_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3434173","issn":["25730142"],"issue":"CSCSW3","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","paperid":"264","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction","title_org":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction - CSCW","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85175611527"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b7937331-5ab4-4ae6-810e-90c2cfc52cb4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Speculative Data Work &amp; Dashboards : Designing Alternative Data Visions","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383945","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"This article analyzes the role of hype in performing and translating corporate sociotechnical imaginaries of digital technologies, into the context of Danish society. Drawing on ethnographic fieldwork of technology events in Denmark, the paper proposes “hot air” as a concept to describe how hype for the future performs these imaginaries. The paper describes the overlapping sociotechnical imaginaries that dominate these events and the performative effects of hype and its critique, in articulating and translating them. The paper makes an empirical and conceptual contribution to the study of sociotechnical imaginaries, in particular their socio-material performance, the role of corporations in articulating them, and how hype is central to their translation.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Hockenhull, Michael","Cohn, Marisa"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384413"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Big Tech","Corporations","Data","Digital transformation","Digitalization","Futures","Hot air","Hype","Sociotechnical imaginaries"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hot air amp corporate sociotechnical imaginaries performing and translating digital futures in the danish tech scene","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86073250/hockenhull_and_cohn_hot_air_and_corporate_sociotechnical_imaginaries_2020_accepted_manuscript_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384413","identifier":[{"id":"85101781619","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-d5fb8f45-fae3-4ec3-98cd-e36fc92523e4"],"keyword":["Big Tech","Digitalization","corporations","data","digital transformation","futures","hot air","hype","sociotechnical imaginaries"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7F87E35516344687F840D98BC8E0376"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86073250/hockenhull_and_cohn_hot_air_and_corporate_sociotechnical_imaginaries_2020_accepted_manuscript_version.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/1461444820929319","issn":["14614448","14617315"],"issn_org":["14614448"],"issue":"2","journal":"New Media & Society","publisher":"Sage Publishing","status":"p","title":"New Media & Society","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101781619"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d5fb8f45-fae3-4ec3-98cd-e36fc92523e4"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Hot air &amp; corporate sociotechnical imaginaries : Performing and translating digital futures in the Danish tech scene","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384413","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"On the eve of the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) coming into effect we, a university laboratory, marked the occasion with an interactive installation called Compliance. Data traces from Compliance were subsequently processed by the lab, here enacted in the form of a play. While much discussion has centered around modern 'black-boxed' processing of data, less attention has been paid to the value of the data itself, and whether it merits use. We draw on dramaturgical methods for both analysis and presentation [15], allowing for readers to imagine staging their own, different, versions of the event. Drawing on the ambiguous ontological status of (yet unexamined) data, we offer a discussion on the value of data, its use and non-use, as well as how to live with this ambivalence, continuously negotiating social contracts about our further conduct with the data.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","Ferreira, Pedro","Cohn, Marisa","Henriksen, Line","Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","Hockenhull, Michael","Cakici, Baki","Blønd, Marie","Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","Zell, Sonja"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384849"],"event":{"acronym":"CHI","title":"ACM Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["GDPR","data non use","dramaturgical methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"upon not opening the black box","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85090243080","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384849","identifier":[{"id":"85090243080","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"keyword":["GDPR","data non-use","dramaturgical methods"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-276723FEC91BFD0CA307B03D82CF6C55"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Simy Kaur","key":"gahooniapau","last":"Gahoonia","locid":"6c543b1d-8137-489d-b444-d17921fc9de9","name":"Gahoonia, Simy Kaur","orcid":"0000-0001-9668-6868","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pedro","key":"ferreirapau","last":"Ferreira","locid":"1b3cff7f-1549-4998-b52e-0ba9093e1d01","name":"Ferreira, Pedro","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marisa","key":"cohnpau","last":"Cohn","locid":"e9d14210-bc71-4824-b7cf-fd1448d022ab","name":"Cohn, Marisa","orcid":"0000-0002-3912-2166","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","name":"Henriksen, Line","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine Meldgaard","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","locid":"0c46864b-a7f8-4439-aeda-0781f77bfe88","name":"Kjær, Katrine Meldgaard","orcid":"0000-0001-7994-5186","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Baki","key":"cakicipau","last":"Cakici","locid":"b614ac6f-eefa-44a3-8b9a-092b88d86d37","name":"Cakici, Baki","orcid":"0000-0002-1039-8177","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marie","key":"bløndpau","last":"Blønd","name":"Blønd, Marie","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Cæcilie Sloth","key":"laursenpau","last":"Laursen","locid":"3ef9211c-34b2-4061-ba59-c5800d6a5b32","name":"Laursen, Cæcilie Sloth","orcid":"0000-0001-7102-7268","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Sonja","key":"zellpau","last":"Zell","name":"Zell, Sonja","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3334480.3381813","eisbn":["9781450368193/20/04"],"pages":"1-9","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"CHI ’20 Extended Abstracts"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85090243080"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f39b7fd5-7493-4bf3-a0f8-14dc03ecc282"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Upon Not Opening The Black Box","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384849","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The data revolution is here, and society is being fundamentally transformed by digitalization. Or so the story goes. Digital technologies and data may have tangibly changed society, but the revolution has been in hype, hot air, and buzzwords. As Big Tech consolidates and monopolizes, speculation runs rampant on the next big disruption. Denmark is at the forefront of public sector digitalization, and its industries are knowledge-intensive and innovative. Professionals from both the public and private sectors mingle to figure out how to seize the data moment. This dissertation seeks to study how such speculation on, with, and about data is related to the ongoing work of digitalization in both the private and public sectors of Denmark – which the dissertation names digitalization work. In order to conduct this study, it draws on a range of disparate research traditions including STS, technofeminist studies, e-Government studies, CSCW, Speculative Design, and Critical Data Studies. They enable an interdisciplinary engagement which identifies what the dissertation calls speculative relations between data and digitalization work. It does so by taking a relational and ethnographic approach which ‘follows’ data as a sociomaterial actor, from the collapse of a public-private big data infrastructure project, through a gyre of tech events filled with hype, to an innovation project in a public sector organization. Through its relational approach, the dissertation is able to trace how data is connected through speculation to the actual work of digitalization. Furthermore, by developing a speculative ethnography, the dissertation not only studies but also engages with speculation as an opportunity for different futures. It does so by exploring its own role in speculation on data, through reflexive enrollment in the field and design practices. In its three research papers, the dissertation redescribes and conceptualizes the role played by speculation on data in digitalization work. The papers identify specific speculative relations by developing concepts such as ‘hot air’ and ‘speculative data work,’ and describe how figures such as ‘digital humanists’ are speculated about. Together, these studies describe how speculation on data precedes digitalization work, and pervades the work practices, infrastructures, and relations to labor such work consists of. The papers also explore how these relations can be intervened in through designing alternative infrastructures and developing more situated critiques. The dissertation thereby contributes original knowledge to the empirical understanding of speculation, data, and how digitalization takes place, and makes a theoretical contribution by bringing research on speculation into contact with research of data, digitalization and work. The dissertation thereby argues for the importance of paying attention to speculation, if we are to understand the actual changes that data and digitalization are causing.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Hockenhull, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383103"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"speculative relations data and digitalization work in the danish public private tech sector","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067199/Dissertation_Michael_Hockenhull_Speculative_Relations.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383103","ids":["itu-7d5c9f9b-618a-49ad-a47a-9e0507256efb"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-339552BBD1B13413A785190F3D706978"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/109067199/Dissertation_Michael_Hockenhull_Speculative_Relations.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Center for Digital Welfare","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Michael","key":"hockenhullpau","last":"Hockenhull","locid":"5041f387-908f-4845-9f79-3a6dbd527f4b","name":"Hockenhull, Michael","orcid":"0000-0002-2670-2102","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788779490406"],"issn":["16023536"],"issue":"171","pages":"202","publisher":"IT-Universitetet i København","series":"ITU-DS","status":"p"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-7d5c9f9b-618a-49ad-a47a-9e0507256efb"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Speculative Relations : Data and Digitalization Work in the Danish Public-Private Tech Sector","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383103","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"This article puts forward a bestiary of digital monsters. By bringing into dialogue scholarship in monster theory with that in science and technology studies, we develop the idea of the bestiary as a way of exploring sites where digital monsters are made. We discuss the role of bestiaries in narrating anxieties about the present. We proceed to populate our bestiary with various sociotechnical ‘beasts’ arising in collaborative research project on new data relations in Denmark. The paper argues for the place of the ever-incomplete bestiary in understanding digital monsters, for the bestiary’s role as a gathering point within our project, and for its capacities to speak beyond a single research setting. Through the bestiary, we look toward the ways we already live with monsters and to the forms of analysis available for describing the beasts in our midst.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Burnett, John Mark","Cohn, Marisa","Gad, Christopher","Hockenhull, Michael","Jørgensen, Bastian","Maguire, James","Ojala, Mace","Winthereik, Brit Ross"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382266"],"event":{"acronym":"IFIP WG82","title":"IFIP WG 8.2 Working Conference"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Digitization","Ethnography","Monsters"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a bestiary of digital monsters","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/83634356/A_Bestiary_of_Digital_Monsters_Final.docm","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382266","identifier":[{"id":"85058283529","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-434ca7be-fac7-4c44-a1d2-399ce958a1d1"],"keyword":["Digitization","ethnography",